WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.630 --> 00:00:09.170
Now we can talk about some shell commands that are the same for everyone once you're logged on to shell

2
00:00:09.530 --> 00:00:13.650
The zos-unix shell is above all a programmer's interface

3
00:00:13.650 --> 00:00:21.370
The shell has many general tools that can help any programmer and in addition there are several commands designed especially for the C programmer

4
00:00:22.210 --> 00:00:24.530
Shell commands are entered on the command line

5
00:00:24.970 --> 00:00:30.210
Shell command options are usually specified by a minus sign followed by a single character

6
00:00:30.210 --> 00:00:36.430
So for example LS minus L some commands except options that have accompanying arguments

7
00:00:36.430 --> 00:00:43.890
For example LS minus L slash user slash lib the argument may be a number a string a file name or something else a

8
00:00:44.590 --> 00:00:48.930
Shell command can be interrupted and stopped by typing escape character and C

9
00:00:49.470 --> 00:00:55.930
Entering a command with minus question mark or entering it incorrectly results in the command syntax being displayed

10
00:00:56.930 --> 00:01:02.130
Once a shell command begins running it has access to the following three files

11
00:01:03.170 --> 00:01:07.410
standard input with a file descriptor or identifier of zero

12
00:01:08.210 --> 00:01:10.710
standard output file descriptor of one and

13
00:01:11.290 --> 00:01:14.210
standard error with the file descriptor of two a

14
00:01:14.650 --> 00:01:20.970
Shell user can redirect the input output and error files to redirect an output to a file

15
00:01:21.370 --> 00:01:29.010
Enter greater than a file name at the end of a command. For example sort minus you file one greater than out file

16
00:01:29.610 --> 00:01:36.090
If the file does not exist it will be created and if it does exist the output writes over any information in the file

17
00:01:36.090 --> 00:01:42.770
Unless greater than greater than file name is specified to append the output to the existing file

18
00:01:43.290 --> 00:01:52.300
The shell commands are a powerful tool for the shell user because multiple commands can be combined on a single command line

19
00:01:52.700 --> 00:02:00.980
To run a series of commands one after another use a semicolon or use double ampersand and double vertical bar

20
00:02:01.340 --> 00:02:07.900
When stringing multiple commands together these characters can be used to control whether or not the second command should be run

21
00:02:07.900 --> 00:02:13.800
If the command proceeds ampersand ampersand then it completes successfully the second command is run

22
00:02:14.300 --> 00:02:20.380
If the command that proceeds double pipe fails the command following double pipe is run

23
00:02:20.900 --> 00:02:25.620
You can also run more than one command concurrently by using a pipe between the commands

24
00:02:25.660 --> 00:02:32.500
Now the idea of a pipeline is that the output from one command is piped in as input to the next command

25
00:02:33.100 --> 00:02:36.900
Many shell commands are well suited to be used in a pipeline

26
00:02:37.660 --> 00:02:46.140
When a command is entered in the shell another command cannot be entered until the shell prompt appears the job runs as a foreground job

27
00:02:46.780 --> 00:02:50.940
Shell commands that take a long time to complete can be run in the background

28
00:02:50.940 --> 00:02:56.540
The shell does not wait for a job running in the background to complete before presenting a prompt

29
00:02:56.540 --> 00:02:59.980
Because you know it's running in the background

30
00:03:01.900 --> 00:03:07.180
When a shell command is entered a new process is started

31
00:03:07.980 --> 00:03:11.500
The shell runs the command in its own process group

32
00:03:11.500 --> 00:03:17.020
It's considered a separate job and the shell assigns it a job identifier

33
00:03:17.900 --> 00:03:21.660
The job identifier is a number known only to the shell

34
00:03:22.380 --> 00:03:31.180
The system assigns the process a process group identifier or PGID and a process identifier PID

35
00:03:32.460 --> 00:03:37.340
When only one command is entered the PGID is the same as the PID

36
00:03:37.900 --> 00:03:43.900
When multiple commands are entered using a pipe several processes with their own PID will be started

37
00:03:45.020 --> 00:03:48.860
All these processes have the same PGID and shell job number

38
00:03:49.820 --> 00:03:56.220
To check the status of shell commands the commands jobs or PS can be used

39
00:03:56.220 --> 00:04:00.700
The jobs command displays the status of currently running background jobs

40
00:04:00.700 --> 00:04:04.940
And the PS command displays the status of currently running processes

41
00:04:05.900 --> 00:04:10.380
A foreground job can be cancelled by entering a state character plus C

42
00:04:11.180 --> 00:04:15.660
A background job is cancelled by using the kill or super kill command

43
00:04:15.660 --> 00:04:19.180
To stop a foreground job enter escape character and Z

44
00:04:19.180 --> 00:04:22.860
To stop a background job use the kill command for this stop option

45
00:04:23.660 --> 00:04:28.220
And a job can be delayed from running until another background job has completed

46
00:04:28.220 --> 00:04:29.740
by using the wait command

47
00:04:31.820 --> 00:04:38.220
The submit command sends a batch job to the primary job entry subsystem or JES for processing

48
00:04:38.860 --> 00:04:44.700
The file name argument can either be a Unix path name data set or input from the standard input

49
00:04:45.580 --> 00:04:50.140
If the submit command is successful the job ID of the submitted job and the file name are

50
00:04:50.140 --> 00:04:55.980
displayed as part of the output written to standard output unless modified by the minus J option

51
00:04:57.020 --> 00:05:00.780
The JCL must have a job card if a job card is not present

52
00:05:00.780 --> 00:05:02.940
Then the submit command will fail

53
00:05:05.180 --> 00:05:13.260
TSO CMD allows you to run a TSOE command from the shell invoking authorized TSOE commands

54
00:05:13.980 --> 00:05:16.220
Invoke it from a shell script or a prompt

55
00:05:16.780 --> 00:05:23.180
It's best that the TSOE command and its parameters be enclosed in quotes to avoid shell parsing

56
00:05:24.140 --> 00:05:30.060
The COS Unix shell uses environment variables to define characteristics of the shell environment

57
00:05:30.940 --> 00:05:35.660
These variables can be modified to a specific installation or user's needs

58
00:05:36.220 --> 00:05:40.140
Using environment variables provides a flexible method for customizing the shell

59
00:05:40.780 --> 00:05:45.740
The system initiates the shell for the user after the user enters a command to log into the shell

60
00:05:46.620 --> 00:05:51.740
During the initialization the system sets environment variables for the user log name,

61
00:05:51.740 --> 00:05:56.060
home, and shell from information in the rackf user profile

62
00:05:56.700 --> 00:06:00.860
Log name is the user ID that remote systems use when diving into your system

63
00:06:01.500 --> 00:06:05.420
Home is a variable for the path name of the user's working directory

64
00:06:05.420 --> 00:06:08.380
And shell identifies the shell to be invoked

65
00:06:09.500 --> 00:06:15.020
Environment variables can be defined in the rackf user profile, the slash etc slash profile,

66
00:06:15.020 --> 00:06:21.260
the dollar home slash dot profile file, and the file name named in the env environment

67
00:06:21.260 --> 00:06:23.820
variable in a shell command or a shell script

68
00:06:23.820 --> 00:06:31.020
The C shell with all its commands and tools provides a real programmer's interface

69
00:06:31.020 --> 00:06:35.580
It's specifically designed to have a syntax similar to the C programming language

70
00:06:36.300 --> 00:06:40.700
The comparison between the standard and the C shell shows which files are related

71
00:06:40.700 --> 00:06:44.620
and which have to be customized from the system programmer's point of view

72
00:06:46.220 --> 00:06:51.100
Okay, that's a wrap on the COS Unix shell and utilities

73
00:06:51.740 --> 00:06:54.860
You can now escape character in Z out of this process

74
00:06:54.860 --> 00:06:58.300
But before you go, let me share a little bit of good news

75
00:06:58.300 --> 00:07:03.740
The next set of modules I've invited some guest speakers to give you a break from me

76
00:07:04.300 --> 00:07:05.500
So enjoy them!
